      "input": null,
      "input_bytes": null,
      "input_storage": null,
      "text": "All four ran. Note the path: the commands as given are relative to the repo root, but my working directory is `scratch-project`, so I ran them against `/home/USER/Desktop/agent-lens/experiments/tracer-bullet/emit.mjs`.\n\n## Byte counts\n\n| Command | Emitted | What reached my context |\n|---|---|---|\n| `emit.mjs 1KB` | 1,024 | 1,024 — full payload inline |\n| `emit.mjs 100KB` | 102,400 | ~2,048 preview + file pointer |\n| `emit.mjs 1MB` | 1,048,576 | ~2,048 preview + file pointer |\n| `emit.mjs 10MB` | 10,485,760 | ~2,048 preview + file pointer |\n\nAll three persisted files are byte-exact (`102400`, `1048576`, `10485760` per `wc -c`).\n\n## The premise in `emit.mjs` doesn't hold here\n\nThe header comment (`emit.mjs:1-8`) says the hook's `tool_output` truncation threshold is probed by reading \"the exact cutoff offset\" from the last surviving marker. This harness doesn't truncate. Over-threshold output is written **whole** to `~/.claude/projects/.../tool-results/<id>.txt` and replaced in-context by a `<persisted-output>` block: a size label, the file path, and a fixed **first-2KB** preview.\n\nConsequences for the Q3 methodology:\n\n- **There is no cutoff offset to recover.** The preview always ends around byte 2048 regardless of whether the payload was 100KB or 10MB — the last visible marker is `<<@000001536>>` in all three cases, identical. The marker stride buys you nothing, because the tail isn't lost, it's relocated.\n- **What the bands actually measure** is the persist/inline decision boundary, not a truncation point. That boundary is somewhere in `(1KB, 100KB]` — the four bands are too coarse to pin it, since 1KB passed inline and the next band up already persisted.\n- **The preview is head-only**, so a probe designed to detect middle-elision or tail-preservation would read as a clean truncation at 2KB and produce a false result.\n\nIf pinning the real threshold is the goal, a bisect over that gap (say 2KB → 64KB) would do it in a handful of runs, and reading the persisted file's tail would confirm losslessness at each step. Want me to run that?",
